= Confirmation bias — Simple =

'''Confirmation bias''' is the tendency to handle information in ways that favor what you already believe or expect.

It can happen when you:

* search mostly for supporting evidence;
* interpret unclear evidence in your preferred direction;
* remember supporting examples more easily;
* judge conflicting evidence more harshly.

Confirmation bias does not mean someone is lying. People can sincerely believe they are being fair.

One way to reduce it is to actively ask what evidence would prove your idea wrong or support a competing explanation.

